#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1614
msgid "Use of alternative formatting styles"
msgstr "Andere Formatierungsstile verwenden"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1616
msgid ""
"When logging was added to the Python standard library, the only way of "
"formatting messages with variable content was to use the %-formatting "
"method. Since then, Python has gained two new formatting "
"approaches: :class:`string.Template` (added in Python 2.4) "
"and :meth:`str.format` (added in Python 2.6)."
msgstr ""
"Als die Protokollierung in die Python-Standardbibliothek aufgenommen wurde, "
"war die %-Formatierung die einzige Möglichkeit, Meldungen mit veränderlichem "
"Inhalt zu formatieren. Seitdem hat Python zwei neue Formatierungsansätze "
"erhalten: :class:`string.Template` (hinzugekommen in Python 2.4) "
"und :meth:`str.format` (hinzugekommen in Python 2.6)."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1622
msgid ""
"Logging (as of 3.2) provides improved support for these two additional "
"formatting styles. The :class:`Formatter` class been enhanced to take an "
"additional, optional keyword parameter named ``style``. This defaults to "
"``'%'``, but other possible values are ``'{'`` and ``'$'``, which correspond "
"to the other two formatting styles. Backwards compatibility is maintained by "
"default (as you would expect), but by explicitly specifying a style "
"parameter, you get the ability to specify format strings which work "
"with :meth:`str.format` or :class:`string.Template`. Here's an example "
"console session to show the possibilities:"
msgstr ""
"Die Protokollierung bietet (seit 3.2) bessere Unterstützung für diese beiden "
"zusätzlichen Formatierungsstile. Die Klasse :class:`Formatter` wurde um "
"einen zusätzlichen, optionalen Schlüsselwortparameter namens ``style`` "
"erweitert. Dessen Vorgabe ist ``'%'``, möglich sind aber auch die Werte "
"``'{'`` und ``'$'``, die den beiden anderen Formatierungsstilen entsprechen. "
"Die Verträglichkeit mit früheren Versionen bleibt standardmäßig erhalten "
"(wie zu erwarten), aber indem du einen style-Parameter ausdrücklich angibst, "
"kannst du Formatzeichenketten verwenden, die mit :meth:`str.format` "
"oder :class:`string.Template` arbeiten. Hier eine Beispielsitzung in der "
"Konsole, die die Möglichkeiten zeigt:"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1632
msgid ""
">>> import logging\n"
">>> root = logging.getLogger()\n"
">>> root.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n"
">>> handler = logging.StreamHandler()\n"
">>> bf = logging.Formatter('{asctime} {name} {levelname:8s} {message}',\n"
"...                        style='{')\n"
">>> handler.setFormatter(bf)\n"
">>> root.addHandler(handler)\n"
">>> logger = logging.getLogger('foo.bar')\n"
">>> logger.debug('This is a DEBUG message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:11:55,341 foo.bar DEBUG    This is a DEBUG message\n"
">>> logger.critical('This is a CRITICAL message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:12:11,526 foo.bar CRITICAL This is a CRITICAL message\n"
">>> df = logging.Formatter('$asctime $name ${levelname} $message',\n"
"...                        style='$')\n"
">>> handler.setFormatter(df)\n"
">>> logger.debug('This is a DEBUG message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:13:06,924 foo.bar DEBUG This is a DEBUG message\n"
">>> logger.critical('This is a CRITICAL message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:13:11,494 foo.bar CRITICAL This is a CRITICAL message\n"
">>>"
msgstr ""
">>> import logging\n"
">>> root = logging.getLogger()\n"
">>> root.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n"
">>> handler = logging.StreamHandler()\n"
">>> bf = logging.Formatter('{asctime} {name} {levelname:8s} {message}',\n"
"...                        style='{')\n"
">>> handler.setFormatter(bf)\n"
">>> root.addHandler(handler)\n"
">>> logger = logging.getLogger('foo.bar')\n"
">>> logger.debug('This is a DEBUG message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:11:55,341 foo.bar DEBUG    This is a DEBUG message\n"
">>> logger.critical('This is a CRITICAL message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:12:11,526 foo.bar CRITICAL This is a CRITICAL message\n"
">>> df = logging.Formatter('$asctime $name ${levelname} $message',\n"
"...                        style='$')\n"
">>> handler.setFormatter(df)\n"
">>> logger.debug('This is a DEBUG message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:13:06,924 foo.bar DEBUG This is a DEBUG message\n"
">>> logger.critical('This is a CRITICAL message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:13:11,494 foo.bar CRITICAL This is a CRITICAL message\n"
">>>"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1656
msgid ""
"Note that the formatting of logging messages for final output to logs is "
"completely independent of how an individual logging message is constructed. "
"That can still use %-formatting, as shown here::"
msgstr ""
"Beachte, dass die Formatierung von Protokollmeldungen für die endgültige "
"Ausgabe in die Protokolle völlig unabhängig davon ist, wie eine einzelne "
"Protokollmeldung aufgebaut wird. Die kann weiterhin %-Formatierung "
"verwenden, wie hier gezeigt::"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1660
msgid ""
">>> logger.error('This is an%s %s %s', 'other,', 'ERROR,', 'message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:19:29,833 foo.bar ERROR This is another, ERROR, message\n"
">>>"
msgstr ""
">>> logger.error('This is an%s %s %s', 'other,', 'ERROR,', 'message')\n"
"2010-10-28 15:19:29,833 foo.bar ERROR This is another, ERROR, message\n"
">>>"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1664
msgid ""
"Logging calls (``logger.debug()``, ``logger.info()`` etc.) only take "
"positional parameters for the actual logging message itself, with keyword "
"parameters used only for determining options for how to handle the actual "
"logging call (e.g. the ``exc_info`` keyword parameter to indicate that "
"traceback information should be logged, or the ``extra`` keyword parameter "
"to indicate additional contextual information to be added to the log). So "
"you cannot directly make logging calls using :meth:`str.format` "
"or :class:`string.Template` syntax, because internally the logging package "
"uses %-formatting to merge the format string and the variable arguments. "
"There would be no changing this while preserving backward compatibility, "
"since all logging calls which are out there in existing code will be using %-"
"format strings."
msgstr ""
"Protokollierungsaufrufe (``logger.debug()``, ``logger.info()`` und so "
"weiter) nehmen für die eigentliche Protokollmeldung nur positionsbezogene "
"Parameter; Schlüsselwortparameter dienen nur dazu, Optionen für die "
"Behandlung des Protokollierungsaufrufs festzulegen (etwa der "
"Schlüsselwortparameter ``exc_info``, der anzeigt, dass Traceback-"
"Informationen protokolliert werden sollen, oder der Schlüsselwortparameter "
"``extra``, der zusätzliche Kontextinformationen für das Protokoll anzeigt). "
"Du kannst Protokollierungsaufrufe also nicht unmittelbar in der Syntax "
"von :meth:`str.format` oder :class:`string.Template` machen, weil das Paket "
"logging intern %-Formatierung verwendet, um Formatzeichenkette und "
"veränderliche Argumente zusammenzuführen. Daran ließe sich nichts ändern, "
"ohne die Verträglichkeit mit früheren Versionen aufzugeben, denn alle "
"Protokollierungsaufrufe im vorhandenen Code verwenden %-Formatzeichenketten."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1677
msgid ""
"There is, however, a way that you can use {}- and $- formatting to construct "
"your individual log messages. Recall that for a message you can use an "
"arbitrary object as a message format string, and that the logging package "
"will call ``str()`` on that object to get the actual format string. Consider "
"the following two classes::"
msgstr ""
"Es gibt allerdings einen Weg, {}- und $-Formatierung zu nutzen, um deine "
"einzelnen Protokollmeldungen aufzubauen. Denk daran, dass du als "
"Formatzeichenkette einer Meldung ein beliebiges Objekt verwenden kannst und "
"dass das Paket logging ``str()`` auf dieses Objekt anwendet, um die "
"eigentliche Formatzeichenkette zu erhalten. Betrachte die folgenden zwei "
"Klassen::"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1683 ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:2771
msgid ""
"class BraceMessage:\n"
"    def __init__(self, fmt, /, *args, **kwargs):\n"
"        self.fmt = fmt\n"
"        self.args = args\n"
"        self.kwargs = kwargs\n"
"\n"
"    def __str__(self):\n"
"        return self.fmt.format(*self.args, **self.kwargs)\n"
"\n"
"class DollarMessage:\n"
"    def __init__(self, fmt, /, **kwargs):\n"
"        self.fmt = fmt\n"
"        self.kwargs = kwargs\n"
"\n"
"    def __str__(self):\n"
"        from string import Template\n"
"        return Template(self.fmt).substitute(**self.kwargs)"
msgstr ""
"class BraceMessage:\n"
"    def __init__(self, fmt, /, *args, **kwargs):\n"
"        self.fmt = fmt\n"
"        self.args = args\n"
"        self.kwargs = kwargs\n"
"\n"
"    def __str__(self):\n"
"        return self.fmt.format(*self.args, **self.kwargs)\n"
"\n"
"class DollarMessage:\n"
"    def __init__(self, fmt, /, **kwargs):\n"
"        self.fmt = fmt\n"
"        self.kwargs = kwargs\n"
"\n"
"    def __str__(self):\n"
"        from string import Template\n"
"        return Template(self.fmt).substitute(**self.kwargs)"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1701
msgid ""
"Either of these can be used in place of a format string, to allow {}- or $-"
"formatting to be used to build the actual \"message\" part which appears in "
"the formatted log output in place of \"%(message)s\" or \"{message}\" or "
"\"$message\". It's a little unwieldy to use the class names whenever you "
"want to log something, but it's quite palatable if you use an alias such as "
"__ (double underscore --- not to be confused with _, the single underscore "
"used as a synonym/alias for :func:`gettext.gettext` or its brethren)."
msgstr ""
"Beide lassen sich anstelle einer Formatzeichenkette verwenden, sodass sich "
"der eigentliche „Meldungs“-Teil, der in der formatierten Protokollausgabe "
"anstelle von „%(message)s“, „{message}“ oder „$message“ erscheint, mit {}- "
"oder $-Formatierung aufbauen lässt. Die Klassennamen jedes Mal zu schreiben, "
"wenn du etwas protokollieren willst, ist etwas sperrig, aber durchaus "
"erträglich, wenn du einen Alias wie __ verwendest (doppelter Unterstrich --- "
"nicht zu verwechseln mit _, dem einfachen Unterstrich, der als Synonym bzw. "
"Alias für :func:`gettext.gettext` und Verwandte dient)."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1709
msgid ""
"The above classes are not included in Python, though they're easy enough to "
"copy and paste into your own code. They can be used as follows (assuming "
"that they're declared in a module called ``wherever``):"
msgstr ""
"Die obigen Klassen sind nicht Teil von Python, lassen sich aber leicht in "
"deinen eigenen Code kopieren. Sie lassen sich so verwenden (unter der "
"Annahme, dass sie in einem Modul namens ``wherever`` deklariert sind):"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1713
msgid ""
">>> from wherever import BraceMessage as __\n"
">>> print(__('Message with {0} {name}', 2, name='placeholders'))\n"
"Message with 2 placeholders\n"
">>> class Point: pass\n"
"...\n"
">>> p = Point()\n"
">>> p.x = 0.5\n"
">>> p.y = 0.5\n"
">>> print(__('Message with coordinates: ({point.x:.2f}, {point.y:.2f})',\n"
"...       point=p))\n"
"Message with coordinates: (0.50, 0.50)\n"
">>> from wherever import DollarMessage as __\n"
">>> print(__('Message with $num $what', num=2, what='placeholders'))\n"
"Message with 2 placeholders\n"
">>>"
msgstr ""
">>> from wherever import BraceMessage as __\n"
">>> print(__('Message with {0} {name}', 2, name='placeholders'))\n"
"Message with 2 placeholders\n"
">>> class Point: pass\n"
"...\n"
">>> p = Point()\n"
">>> p.x = 0.5\n"
">>> p.y = 0.5\n"
">>> print(__('Message with coordinates: ({point.x:.2f}, {point.y:.2f})',\n"
"...       point=p))\n"
"Message with coordinates: (0.50, 0.50)\n"
">>> from wherever import DollarMessage as __\n"
">>> print(__('Message with $num $what', num=2, what='placeholders'))\n"
"Message with 2 placeholders\n"
">>>"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1731
msgid ""
"While the above examples use ``print()`` to show how the formatting works, "
"you would of course use ``logger.debug()`` or similar to actually log using "
"this approach."
msgstr ""
"Die obigen Beispiele verwenden ``print()``, um die Arbeitsweise der "
"Formatierung zu zeigen. Zum eigentlichen Protokollieren mit diesem Ansatz "
"würdest du natürlich ``logger.debug()`` oder Ähnliches verwenden."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1735
msgid ""
"One thing to note is that you pay no significant performance penalty with "
"this approach: the actual formatting happens not when you make the logging "
"call, but when (and if) the logged message is actually about to be output to "
"a log by a handler. So the only slightly unusual thing which might trip you "
"up is that the parentheses go around the format string and the arguments, "
"not just the format string. That's because the __ notation is just syntax "
"sugar for a constructor call to one of the :samp:`{XXX}Message` classes."
msgstr ""
"Festzuhalten ist, dass du mit diesem Ansatz keinen bedeutsamen "
"Geschwindigkeitsnachteil hast: Die eigentliche Formatierung findet nicht "
"beim Protokollierungsaufruf statt, sondern dann (und nur dann), wenn die "
"protokollierte Meldung von einem Handler tatsächlich in ein Protokoll "
"geschrieben werden soll. Das einzige leicht Ungewöhnliche, über das du "
"stolpern könntest, ist, dass die Klammern um die Formatzeichenkette *und* "
"die Argumente stehen, nicht nur um die Formatzeichenkette. Das liegt daran, "
"dass die __-Schreibweise nur Zuckerguss für einen Konstruktoraufruf einer "
"der Klassen :samp:`{XXX}Message` ist."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1743
msgid ""
"If you prefer, you can use a :class:`LoggerAdapter` to achieve a similar "
"effect to the above, as in the following example::"
msgstr ""
"Wenn du magst, kannst du mit einem :class:`LoggerAdapter` eine ähnliche "
"Wirkung wie oben erreichen, wie im folgenden Beispiel::"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1746
msgid ""
"import logging\n"
"\n"
"class Message:\n"
"    def __init__(self, fmt, args):\n"
"        self.fmt = fmt\n"
"        self.args = args\n"
"\n"
"    def __str__(self):\n"
"        return self.fmt.format(*self.args)\n"
"\n"
"class StyleAdapter(logging.LoggerAdapter):\n"
"    def log(self, level, msg, /, *args, stacklevel=1, **kwargs):\n"
"        if self.isEnabledFor(level):\n"
"            msg, kwargs = self.process(msg, kwargs)\n"
"            self.logger.log(level, Message(msg, args), **kwargs,\n"
"                            stacklevel=stacklevel+1)\n"
"\n"
"logger = StyleAdapter(logging.getLogger(__name__))\n"
"\n"
"def main():\n"
"    logger.debug('Hello, {}', 'world!')\n"
"\n"
"if __name__ == '__main__':\n"
"    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG)\n"
"    main()"
msgstr ""
"import logging\n"
"\n"
"class Message:\n"
"    def __init__(self, fmt, args):\n"
"        self.fmt = fmt\n"
"        self.args = args\n"
"\n"
"    def __str__(self):\n"
"        return self.fmt.format(*self.args)\n"
"\n"
"class StyleAdapter(logging.LoggerAdapter):\n"
"    def log(self, level, msg, /, *args, stacklevel=1, **kwargs):\n"
"        if self.isEnabledFor(level):\n"
"            msg, kwargs = self.process(msg, kwargs)\n"
"            self.logger.log(level, Message(msg, args), **kwargs,\n"
"                            stacklevel=stacklevel+1)\n"
"\n"
"logger = StyleAdapter(logging.getLogger(__name__))\n"
"\n"
"def main():\n"
"    logger.debug('Hello, {}', 'world!')\n"
"\n"
"if __name__ == '__main__':\n"
"    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG)\n"
"    main()"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1772
msgid ""
"The above script should log the message ``Hello, world!`` when run with "
"Python 3.8 or later."
msgstr ""
"Das obige Skript sollte die Meldung ``Hello, world!`` protokollieren, wenn "
"es mit Python 3.8 oder neuer ausgeführt wird."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1781
msgid "Customizing ``LogRecord``"
msgstr "``LogRecord`` anpassen"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1783
msgid ""
"Every logging event is represented by a :class:`LogRecord` instance. When an "
"event is logged and not filtered out by a logger's level, "
"a :class:`LogRecord` is created, populated with information about the event "
"and then passed to the handlers for that logger (and its ancestors, up to "
"and including the logger where further propagation up the hierarchy is "
"disabled). Before Python 3.2, there were only two places where this creation "
"was done:"
msgstr ""
"Jedes Protokollierungsereignis wird durch eine :class:`LogRecord`-Instanz "
"dargestellt. Wird ein Ereignis protokolliert und nicht durch den Schweregrad "
"eines Loggers herausgefiltert, so wird ein :class:`LogRecord` erzeugt, mit "
"Informationen über das Ereignis gefüllt und dann an die Handler dieses "
"Loggers übergeben (und an die seiner Vorfahren, bis einschließlich zu dem "
"Logger, bei dem die weitere Weitergabe in der Hierarchie abgeschaltet ist). "
"Vor Python 3.2 gab es nur zwei Stellen, an denen diese Erzeugung geschah:"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1790
msgid ""
":meth:`Logger.makeRecord`, which is called in the normal process of logging "
"an event. This invoked :class:`LogRecord` directly to create an instance."
msgstr ""
":meth:`Logger.makeRecord`, die im normalen Ablauf der Protokollierung eines "
"Ereignisses aufgerufen wird. Sie rief :class:`LogRecord` unmittelbar auf, um "
"eine Instanz zu erzeugen."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1793
msgid ""
":func:`makeLogRecord`, which is called with a dictionary containing "
"attributes to be added to the LogRecord. This is typically invoked when a "
"suitable dictionary has been received over the network (e.g. in pickle form "
"via a :class:`~handlers.SocketHandler`, or in JSON form via "
"an :class:`~handlers.HTTPHandler`)."
msgstr ""
":func:`makeLogRecord`, die mit einem Dictionary aufgerufen wird, das "
"Attribute enthält, die dem LogRecord hinzugefügt werden sollen. Das wird "
"üblicherweise aufgerufen, wenn ein passendes Dictionary über das Netz "
"empfangen wurde (etwa in pickle-Form über "
"einen :class:`~handlers.SocketHandler` oder in JSON-Form über "
"einen :class:`~handlers.HTTPHandler`)."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1799
msgid ""
"This has usually meant that if you need to do anything special with "
"a :class:`LogRecord`, you've had to do one of the following."
msgstr ""
"Das bedeutete meist, dass du eines der Folgenden tun musstest, wenn du mit "
"einem :class:`LogRecord` etwas Besonderes vorhattest."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1802
msgid ""
"Create your own :class:`Logger` subclass, which "
"overrides :meth:`Logger.makeRecord`, and set it "
"using :func:`~logging.setLoggerClass` before any loggers that you care about "
"are instantiated."
msgstr ""
"Eine eigene Unterklasse von :class:`Logger` anlegen, "
"die :meth:`Logger.makeRecord` überschreibt, und sie "
"mit :func:`~logging.setLoggerClass` setzen, bevor irgendeiner der Logger, um "
"die es dir geht, angelegt wird."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1805
msgid ""
"Add a :class:`Filter` to a logger or handler, which does the necessary "
"special manipulation you need when its :meth:`~Filter.filter` method is "
"called."
msgstr ""
"Einem Logger oder Handler einen :class:`Filter` hinzufügen, der beim Aufruf "
"seiner Methode :meth:`~Filter.filter` die nötige besondere Bearbeitung "
"vornimmt."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1809
msgid ""
"The first approach would be a little unwieldy in the scenario where (say) "
"several different libraries wanted to do different things. Each would "
"attempt to set its own :class:`Logger` subclass, and the one which did this "
"last would win."
msgstr ""
"Der erste Ansatz wäre etwas sperrig in dem Fall, dass (sagen wir) mehrere "
"verschiedene Bibliotheken Unterschiedliches vorhaben. Jede würde versuchen, "
"ihre eigene Unterklasse von :class:`Logger` zu setzen, und die, die das "
"zuletzt täte, würde gewinnen."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1814
msgid ""
"The second approach works reasonably well for many cases, but does not allow "
"you to e.g. use a specialized subclass of :class:`LogRecord`. Library "
"developers can set a suitable filter on their loggers, but they would have "
"to remember to do this every time they introduced a new logger (which they "
"would do simply by adding new packages or modules and doing ::"
msgstr ""
"Der zweite Ansatz funktioniert in vielen Fällen recht gut, erlaubt dir aber "
"zum Beispiel nicht, eine besondere Unterklasse von :class:`LogRecord` zu "
"verwenden. Bibliotheksentwickler können einen passenden Filter an ihren "
"Loggern setzen, müssten aber daran denken, das jedes Mal zu tun, wenn sie "
"einen neuen Logger einführen (was sie schon dadurch tun, dass sie neue "
"Pakete oder Module hinzufügen und ::"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1820
msgid "logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)"
msgstr "logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1822
msgid ""
"at module level). It's probably one too many things to think about. "
"Developers could also add the filter to a :class:`~logging.NullHandler` "
"attached to their top-level logger, but this would not be invoked if an "
"application developer attached a handler to a lower-level library logger --- "
"so output from that handler would not reflect the intentions of the library "
"developer."
msgstr ""
"auf Modulebene schreiben). Das ist vermutlich eine Sache zu viel, an die man "
"denken muss. Entwickler könnten den Filter auch "
"einem :class:`~logging.NullHandler` hinzufügen, der an ihrem obersten Logger "
"hängt, aber der würde nicht aufgerufen, wenn ein Anwendungsentwickler einen "
"Handler an einen tieferliegenden Bibliotheks-Logger hängt --- die Ausgabe "
"dieses Handlers würde dann also nicht die Absichten des "
"Bibliotheksentwicklers widerspiegeln."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1828
msgid ""
"In Python 3.2 and later, :class:`~logging.LogRecord` creation is done "
"through a factory, which you can specify. The factory is just a callable you "
"can set with :func:`~logging.setLogRecordFactory`, and interrogate "
"with :func:`~logging.getLogRecordFactory`. The factory is invoked with the "
"same signature as the :class:`~logging.LogRecord` constructor, "
"as :class:`LogRecord` is the default setting for the factory."
msgstr ""
"In Python 3.2 und neuer geschieht die Erzeugung "
"von :class:`~logging.LogRecord` über eine Factory, die du angeben kannst. "
"Die Factory ist einfach ein aufrufbares Objekt, das du "
"mit :func:`~logging.setLogRecordFactory` setzen und "
"mit :func:`~logging.getLogRecordFactory` abfragen kannst. Die Factory wird "
"mit derselben Signatur wie der Konstruktor von :class:`~logging.LogRecord` "
"aufgerufen, denn :class:`LogRecord` ist die Standardeinstellung für die "
"Factory."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1835
msgid ""
"This approach allows a custom factory to control all aspects of LogRecord "
"creation. For example, you could return a subclass, or just add some "
"additional attributes to the record once created, using a pattern similar to "
"this::"
msgstr ""
"Dieser Ansatz erlaubt einer eigenen Factory, alle Aspekte der Erzeugung "
"eines LogRecord zu steuern. Du könntest zum Beispiel eine Unterklasse "
"zurückgeben oder dem erzeugten Datensatz einfach einige zusätzliche "
"Attribute hinzufügen, nach einem Muster wie diesem::"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1839
msgid ""
"old_factory = logging.getLogRecordFactory()\n"
"\n"
"def record_factory(*args, **kwargs):\n"
"    record = old_factory(*args, **kwargs)\n"
"    record.custom_attribute = 0xdecafbad\n"
"    return record\n"
"\n"
"logging.setLogRecordFactory(record_factory)"
msgstr ""
"old_factory = logging.getLogRecordFactory()\n"
"\n"
"def record_factory(*args, **kwargs):\n"
"    record = old_factory(*args, **kwargs)\n"
"    record.custom_attribute = 0xdecafbad\n"
"    return record\n"
"\n"
"logging.setLogRecordFactory(record_factory)"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1848
msgid ""
"This pattern allows different libraries to chain factories together, and as "
"long as they don't overwrite each other's attributes or unintentionally "
"overwrite the attributes provided as standard, there should be no surprises. "
"However, it should be borne in mind that each link in the chain adds run-"
"time overhead to all logging operations, and the technique should only be "
"used when the use of a :class:`Filter` does not provide the desired result."
msgstr ""
"Dieses Muster erlaubt verschiedenen Bibliotheken, Factorys "
"aneinanderzuketten, und solange sie nicht die Attribute der jeweils anderen "
"oder versehentlich die standardmäßig vorhandenen Attribute überschreiben, "
"sollte es keine Überraschungen geben. Allerdings ist zu bedenken, dass jedes "
"Glied der Kette allen Protokollierungsoperationen Laufzeitaufwand hinzufügt. "
"Die Technik sollte nur dann eingesetzt werden, wenn ein :class:`Filter` "
"nicht das gewünschte Ergebnis liefert."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1860
msgid "Subclassing QueueHandler and QueueListener- a ZeroMQ example"
msgstr ""
"Unterklassen von QueueHandler und QueueListener bilden – ein ZeroMQ-Beispiel"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1863 ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1996
msgid "Subclass ``QueueHandler``"
msgstr "Unterklasse von ``QueueHandler``"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1865
msgid ""
"You can use a :class:`QueueHandler` subclass to send messages to other kinds "
"of queues, for example a ZeroMQ 'publish' socket. In the example below,the "
"socket is created separately and passed to the handler (as its 'queue')::"
msgstr ""
"Mit einer Unterklasse von :class:`QueueHandler` kannst du Meldungen an "
"andere Arten von Warteschlangen senden, etwa an ein „publish“-Socket von "
"ZeroMQ. Im Beispiel unten wird das Socket getrennt erzeugt und dem Handler "
"(als seine „queue“) übergeben::"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1869
msgid ""
"import zmq   # using pyzmq, the Python binding for ZeroMQ\n"
"import json  # for serializing records portably\n"
"\n"
"ctx = zmq.Context()\n"
"sock = zmq.Socket(ctx, zmq.PUB)  # or zmq.PUSH, or other suitable value\n"
"sock.bind('tcp://*:5556')        # or wherever\n"
"\n"
"class ZeroMQSocketHandler(QueueHandler):\n"
"    def enqueue(self, record):\n"
"        self.queue.send_json(record.__dict__)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"handler = ZeroMQSocketHandler(sock)"
msgstr ""
"import zmq   # using pyzmq, the Python binding for ZeroMQ\n"
"import json  # for serializing records portably\n"
"\n"
"ctx = zmq.Context()\n"
"sock = zmq.Socket(ctx, zmq.PUB)  # or zmq.PUSH, or other suitable value\n"
"sock.bind('tcp://*:5556')        # or wherever\n"
"\n"
"class ZeroMQSocketHandler(QueueHandler):\n"
"    def enqueue(self, record):\n"
"        self.queue.send_json(record.__dict__)\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"handler = ZeroMQSocketHandler(sock)"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1884
msgid ""
"Of course there are other ways of organizing this, for example passing in "
"the data needed by the handler to create the socket::"
msgstr ""
"Natürlich gibt es andere Wege, das zu gestalten, etwa indem die Daten "
"übergeben werden, die der Handler zum Erzeugen des Sockets braucht::"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1887
msgid ""
"class ZeroMQSocketHandler(QueueHandler):\n"
"    def __init__(self, uri, socktype=zmq.PUB, ctx=None):\n"
"        self.ctx = ctx or zmq.Context()\n"
"        socket = zmq.Socket(self.ctx, socktype)\n"
"        socket.bind(uri)\n"
"        super().__init__(socket)\n"
"\n"
"    def enqueue(self, record):\n"
"        self.queue.send_json(record.__dict__)\n"
"\n"
"    def close(self):\n"
"        self.queue.close()"
msgstr ""
"class ZeroMQSocketHandler(QueueHandler):\n"
"    def __init__(self, uri, socktype=zmq.PUB, ctx=None):\n"
"        self.ctx = ctx or zmq.Context()\n"
"        socket = zmq.Socket(self.ctx, socktype)\n"
"        socket.bind(uri)\n"
"        super().__init__(socket)\n"
"\n"
"    def enqueue(self, record):\n"
"        self.queue.send_json(record.__dict__)\n"
"\n"
"    def close(self):\n"
"        self.queue.close()"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1902 ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1932
msgid "Subclass ``QueueListener``"
msgstr "Unterklasse von ``QueueListener``"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1904
msgid ""
"You can also subclass :class:`QueueListener` to get messages from other "
"kinds of queues, for example a ZeroMQ 'subscribe' socket. Here's an example::"
msgstr ""
"Du kannst auch eine Unterklasse von :class:`QueueListener` bilden, um "
"Meldungen aus anderen Arten von Warteschlangen zu holen, etwa aus einem "
"„subscribe“-Socket von ZeroMQ. Hier ein Beispiel::"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1907
msgid ""
"class ZeroMQSocketListener(QueueListener):\n"
"    def __init__(self, uri, /, *handlers, **kwargs):\n"
"        self.ctx = kwargs.get('ctx') or zmq.Context()\n"
"        socket = zmq.Socket(self.ctx, zmq.SUB)\n"
"        socket.setsockopt_string(zmq.SUBSCRIBE, '')  # subscribe to "
"everything\n"
"        socket.connect(uri)\n"
"        super().__init__(socket, *handlers, **kwargs)\n"
"\n"
"    def dequeue(self):\n"
"        msg = self.queue.recv_json()\n"
"        return logging.makeLogRecord(msg)"
msgstr ""
"class ZeroMQSocketListener(QueueListener):\n"
"    def __init__(self, uri, /, *handlers, **kwargs):\n"
"        self.ctx = kwargs.get('ctx') or zmq.Context()\n"
"        socket = zmq.Socket(self.ctx, zmq.SUB)\n"
"        socket.setsockopt_string(zmq.SUBSCRIBE, '')  # subscribe to "
"everything\n"
"        socket.connect(uri)\n"
"        super().__init__(socket, *handlers, **kwargs)\n"
"\n"
"    def dequeue(self):\n"
"        msg = self.queue.recv_json()\n"
"        return logging.makeLogRecord(msg)"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1922
msgid "Subclassing QueueHandler and QueueListener- a ``pynng`` example"
msgstr ""
"Unterklassen von QueueHandler und QueueListener bilden – ein ``pynng``-"
"Beispiel"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1924
msgid ""
"In a similar way to the above section, we can implement a listener and "
"handler using :pypi:`pynng`, which is a Python binding to `NNG <https://"
"nng.nanomsg.org/>`_, billed as a spiritual successor to ZeroMQ. The "
"following snippets illustrate -- you can test them in an environment which "
"has ``pynng`` installed. Just for variety, we present the listener first."
msgstr ""
"Ähnlich wie im obigen Abschnitt können wir einen Listener und einen Handler "
"mit :pypi:`pynng` umsetzen, einer Python-Anbindung an `NNG <https://"
"nng.nanomsg.org/>`_, das als geistiger Nachfolger von ZeroMQ angekündigt "
"wird. Die folgenden Ausschnitte zeigen das -- du kannst sie in einer "
"Umgebung testen, in der ``pynng`` installiert ist. Der Abwechslung halber "
"stellen wir den Listener zuerst vor."

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:1934
msgid ""
"# listener.py\n"
"import json\n"
"import logging\n"
"import logging.handlers\n"
"\n"
"import pynng\n"
"\n"
"DEFAULT_ADDR = \"tcp://localhost:13232\"\n"
"\n"
"interrupted = False\n"
"\n"
"class NNGSocketListener(logging.handlers.QueueListener):\n"
"\n"
"    def __init__(self, uri, /, *handlers, **kwargs):\n"
"        # Have a timeout for interruptibility, and open a\n"
"        # subscriber socket\n"
"        socket = pynng.Sub0(listen=uri, recv_timeout=500)\n"
"        # The b'' subscription matches all topics\n"
"        topics = kwargs.pop('topics', None) or b''\n"
"        socket.subscribe(topics)\n"
"        # We treat the socket as a queue\n"
"        super().__init__(socket, *handlers, **kwargs)\n"
"\n"
"    def dequeue(self, block):\n"
"        data = None\n"
"        # Keep looping while not interrupted and no data received over the\n"
"        # socket\n"
"        while not interrupted:\n"
"            try:\n"
"                data = self.queue.recv(block=block)\n"
"                break\n"
"            except pynng.Timeout:\n"
"                pass\n"
"            except pynng.Closed:  # sometimes happens when you hit Ctrl-C\n"
"                break\n"
"        if data is None:\n"
"            return None\n"
"        # Get the logging event sent from a publisher\n"
"        event = json.loads(data.decode('utf-8'))\n"
"        return logging.makeLogRecord(event)\n"
"\n"
"    def enqueue_sentinel(self):\n"
"        # Not used in this implementation, as the socket isn't really a\n"
"        # queue\n"
"        pass\n"
"\n"
"logging.getLogger('pynng').propagate = False\n"
"listener = NNGSocketListener(DEFAULT_ADDR, logging.StreamHandler(), "
"topics=b'')\n"
"listener.start()\n"
"print('Press Ctrl-C to stop.')\n"
"try:\n"
"    while True:\n"
"        pass\n"
"except KeyboardInterrupt:\n"
"    interrupted = True\n"
"finally:\n"
"    listener.stop()"
msgstr ""
"# listener.py\n"
"import json\n"
"import logging\n"
"import logging.handlers\n"
"\n"
"import pynng\n"
"\n"
"DEFAULT_ADDR = \"tcp://localhost:13232\"\n"
"\n"
"interrupted = False\n"
"\n"
"class NNGSocketListener(logging.handlers.QueueListener):\n"
"\n"
"    def __init__(self, uri, /, *handlers, **kwargs):\n"
"        # Have a timeout for interruptibility, and open a\n"
"        # subscriber socket\n"
"        socket = pynng.Sub0(listen=uri, recv_timeout=500)\n"
"        # The b'' subscription matches all topics\n"
"        topics = kwargs.pop('topics', None) or b''\n"
"        socket.subscribe(topics)\n"
"        # We treat the socket as a queue\n"
"        super().__init__(socket, *handlers, **kwargs)\n"
"\n"
"    def dequeue(self, block):\n"
"        data = None\n"
"        # Keep looping while not interrupted and no data received over the\n"
"        # socket\n"
"        while not interrupted:\n"
"            try:\n"
"                data = self.queue.recv(block=block)\n"
"                break\n"
"            except pynng.Timeout:\n"
"                pass\n"
"            except pynng.Closed:  # sometimes happens when you hit Ctrl-C\n"
"                break\n"
"        if data is None:\n"
"            return None\n"
"        # Get the logging event sent from a publisher\n"
"        event = json.loads(data.decode('utf-8'))\n"
"        return logging.makeLogRecord(event)\n"
"\n"
"    def enqueue_sentinel(self):\n"
"        # Not used in this implementation, as the socket isn't really a\n"
"        # queue\n"
"        pass\n"
"\n"
"logging.getLogger('pynng').propagate = False\n"
"listener = NNGSocketListener(DEFAULT_ADDR, logging.StreamHandler(), "
"topics=b'')\n"
"listener.start()\n"
"print('Press Ctrl-C to stop.')\n"
"try:\n"
"    while True:\n"
"        pass\n"
"except KeyboardInterrupt:\n"
"    interrupted = True\n"
"finally:\n"
"    listener.stop()"

#: ../../howto/logging-cookbook.rst:2000
msgid ""
"# sender.py\n"
"import json\n"
"import logging\n"
"import logging.handlers\n"
"import time\n"
"import random\n"
"\n"
"import pynng\n"
"\n"
"DEFAULT_ADDR = \"tcp://localhost:13232\"\n"
"\n"
"class NNGSocketHandler(logging.handlers.QueueHandler):\n"
"\n"
"    def __init__(self, uri):\n"
"        socket = pynng.Pub0(dial=uri, send_timeout=500)\n"
"        super().__init__(socket)\n"
"\n"
"    def enqueue(self, record):\n"
"        # Send the record as UTF-8 encoded JSON\n"
"        d = dict(record.__dict__)\n"
"        data = json.dumps(d)\n"
"        self.queue.send(data.encode('utf-8'))\n"
"\n"
"    def close(self):\n"
"        self.queue.close()\n"
"\n"
"logging.getLogger('pynng').propagate = False\n"
"handler = NNGSocketHandler(DEFAULT_ADDR)\n"
"# Make sure the process ID is in the output\n"
"logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG,\n"
"                    handlers=[logging.StreamHandler(), handler],\n"
"                    format='%(levelname)-8s %(name)10s %(process)6s %"
"(message)s')\n"
"levels = (logging.DEBUG, logging.INFO, logging.WARNING, logging.ERROR,\n"
"          logging.CRITICAL)\n"
"logger_names = ('myapp', 'myapp.lib1', 'myapp.lib2')\n"
"msgno = 1\n"
"while True:\n"
"    # Just randomly select some loggers and levels and log away\n"
"    level = random.choice(levels)\n"
"    logger = logging.getLogger(random.choice(logger_names))\n"
"    logger.log(level, 'Message no. %5d' % msgno)\n"
"    msgno += 1\n"
"    delay = random.random() * 2 + 0.5\n"
"    time.sleep(delay)"
